00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:01.350 align:start position:0%
 
today if you notice we're all wearing  red why it's national wear red day when  everybody's encouraged to wear red to  bring awareness to women's heart health  cardiovascular disease is the number one  killer of women in this country did you  know that this morning we're looking at  sudden cardiac arrest that means an  abrupt loss of heart function every year  more than 350 000 out of hospital  cardiac arrest occur in this country and  the studies show that women are less  likely than men to receive cpr from a  bystander why and less likely to survive  a cardiac event cbs news senior medical  correspondent that's dr tara narula who  is a cardiologist with northwell health  has the story of one woman who suffered  a sudden cardiac arrest at a very young  age and credit cpr was saving her life  happy birthday to you  celebrating another year of life is  something jenny lynn carpio does not  take for granted  in 2005 she almost died  i was 22 years old when i became a new  mom  i was a new wife and i was a college  student jenny lynn was visiting her  mother jocelyn with her then  two-month-old daughter when she decided  to take a nap and  next thing i remember  my mom frantically  pumping at my chest yelling at me to  wake up  police officer was shining a light in my  eyes and my mom had said that  you just experienced a sudden cardiac  arrest  jocelyn was trained in cpr but she never  expected that she would use it to save  her own daughter's life seeing my  daughter having that thing is kind of  scary  about 90 percent of people who suffer  out of hospital cardiac arrest die but  performing cpr more than doubles  someone's chance of survival at the  hospital doctors discovered jenny lynn  had an undiagnosed genetic heart  condition that can cause irregular heart  rhythms putting her at risk for sudden  cardiac death i was shocked i had to  process  that this was going to be a lifelong  thing  surgeons implanted a defibrillator in  jenny lynn to detect and shock her heart  when abnormal and dangerous heart  rhythms are detected she has now had  four defibrillator replacement surgeries  the most recent in 2020.  with this device jennylin is living a  near normal life and using lessons  learned from her own experience to bring  awareness to sudden cardiac arrest and  the importance of cpr what i would like  other people to know in regards to  sending cardiac arrest  is that it can happen at any time  to anybody and that knowing cpr  is a life-saving skill  and  that is why i'm here today  jenny lynn carpio is now a volunteer  spokeswoman for the american heart  association and she hopes her story will  help encourage others to learn  cpr it's so jarring that it happened to  her at such a young age it is it is and  that's what's so you know you heard the  word sudden cardiac arrest yeah many  times there is not a warning sign and so  it is important to know your risk  factors and your family history but also  to know how to do cpr so you can act in  that moment but are there warning signs  so sometimes there can be and you know  things like chest pain or shortness of  breath lightheadedness passing out or  palpitations you know we in cardiology  always encourage people if you  experience any of these you really need  to pay attention to them don't downplay  them or minimize them and bring them up  to your doctor is cardiac arrest the  same as a heart attack that's a great  question and there's a lot of confusion  around that they are not the same thing  so a heart attack can sometimes trigger  a sudden cardiac arrest  but basically the heart attack means  there's a blockage in an artery that  supplies blood to the heart it's and in  that moment the heart cells can die the  heart muscle dies cardiac arrest is an  electrical problem so essentially the  heart goes into an abnormal or irregular  rhythm and arrhythmia and it's not  pumping so the failure of the heart to  pump means no blood is getting to the  brain getting to the organs you can lose  consciousness and really without  treatment you pretty much will die  within minutes how do you help someone  that's having a cardiac event so this is  the most important question so you know  we try to educate people call 9-1-1 and  start performing cpr it's so easy now to  learn how to do hands-only cpr and  really you can as you heard in the piece  increase someone's chance of survival if  you start bystander cpr before even ems  gets there and also improve their  chances of having a good neurologic  outcome because you start perfusing or  providing blood to the brain and so we  tell people you know push hard and fast  in the center of the chest around 100 to  120 beats per minute  to the song staying alive by the bee  gees in your head that's what you want  to think about a little irony there but  it also works right but why are  bystanders that we said in the lead why  are bystanders less likely to help women  isn't that cpr isn't that a terrible  fact yes so  one of the issues is a lot of times we  think well it's probably not her heart  right if a woman's down on the ground it  must not be heart disease and so that's  one thing another is they are worried  about being inappropriate by touching  her  they're worried that they might hurt her  but really you know three quarters of  these out of hospital arrests are  occurring at home and you know it's so  important to learn this imagine you're  there with your loved one they're on the  ground and you feel helpless but you  don't have to right that's the point of  this segment is to empower people learn  how to do cpr but also if you have  access to a defibrillator this can be  life-saving and they're so easy to use  they walk you through every step you  just push the button it tells you where  to push the pad put the pads and you can  really save someone's life how do you  reduce the risk right so there are risk  factors and this is important to know is  that the majority of patients who have a  sudden cardiac arrest have underlying  structural heart disease most often 70  it's coronary heart disease so coronary  artery disease the other 10 to 15 is  something structurally wrong with the  heart muscle some sort of thickening or  scarring or weakening or something wrong  with the blood vessels and then the last  about 10  are some of these genetic abnormalities  that can trigger arrhythmias like you  heard in the piece with genuine which is  why it's important to know your family  history and to know your risk factors  for heart disease  age was a factor but she was 22. right  so it does increase the incidence of  sudden cardiac arrest increases the  older you get but certainly we see this  as she said in every population every  age both genders all right very good to  see you you look nice and red we've all  got the home right i think it's  important i think it's important to send  this message that we're sending today  leading cause of death for women in this  country  people need to understand that  thank you very much tara to learn more  about how to properly perform the  hands-only cpr and use one of those  portable defibrillators go to our cbs  morning's facebook twitter and instagram  page on cbsmornings.com you can watch  tara there she is a demonstration with  tara and an american heart association  trainer they say anybody can learn to do  this

